\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the fidelity attainable by a channel that leaks \emph{nothing} about its
input. An observer is a map $\obs : S \to M$ from a finite configuration space to
a finite record alphabet; it is \emph{perfectly private} when $\obs$ is constant,
so that the record is statistically independent of the configuration. Paired with
a decoder $\dec : M \to S$ and an integer distortion $d$, such a channel realizes
exactly the reconstructions of a single fixed codeword, and the optimal worst-case
distortion is the covering radius of the one-codeword code,
$R(d) = \min_c \max_s d(c,s)$. We prove four groups of results. (i)~Under expected
rather than almost-sure distortion, a perfectly private channel---deterministic or
randomized---attains expected distortion $D$ against a source law $p$ iff a single
reconstruction does, so the optimum is the private rate--distortion function
$\Dpriv(p)=\min_c\Emean_p[d(c,X)]\le R(d)$; for binary configurations with Hamming
distortion the optimizer is the coordinatewise majority vote and
$\Dpriv(p)=\sum_i\min(\mass_i(0),\mass_i(1))$, which for the uniform source equals
$|\alpha|/2$. (ii)~A measure-theoretic (Fano-type) converse: if every distortion
ball has $p$-mass at most $\beta$ and the decoder is correct on a set $G$, then
$p(G)\le \rate(\obs)\cdot\beta$, hence $1-\varepsilon \le \rate\cdot\beta$ under
excess-distortion probability $\varepsilon$; for the uniform binary source this
reads $(1-\varepsilon)2^{|\alpha|}\le\rate\cdot\sum_{i\le D}\binom{|\alpha|}{i}$,
whose zero-slack, unit-rate corner re-derives the sharp threshold.
(iii)~Modulo the full relabeling group, the orbit distortion equals the Hamming
weight gap, the private threshold is exactly $\lceil |\alpha|/2\rceil$, orbit balls
have exact binomial-layer volumes, and the rate converse survives the quotient.
(iv)~For an additive distortion on a product, $R(\sum_i d_i)=\sum_i R(d_i)$ and
$\Dpriv(p)=\sum_i\Dpriv(\marg_i(p))$ with no independence assumption.
For $T$-step histories of a directed network on $n$ participants the private
worst-case distortion is $Tn^2$, the private expected distortion against the
uniform source is $Tn^2/2$, and the relabeled worst-case distortion is
$\lceil Tn^2/2\rceil$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

A surveillance network records a \emph{history}: for each of $T$ time steps and
each ordered pair drawn from $n$ participants, one bit saying whether an
interaction occurred. The history is a point of $\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$ with
$|\alpha| = Tn^2$. Publishing the history is useful and dangerous; the design
question is what a publication channel can achieve subject to a disclosure
constraint.

We take the disclosure constraint to its extreme. A channel is \emph{perfectly
private} if its output is independent of its input---the strongest conceivable
non-disclosure guarantee, and the natural boundary case against which weaker
guarantees should be calibrated. The question is: what fidelity can a channel
that leaks nothing still deliver? The answer is purely combinatorial and exact in
every regime we consider.

\subsection{The collapse principle}

A perfectly private observer emits the same record whatever the world does, so
the decoder's output is a single point $c \in S$ chosen in advance. Joint
optimization of encoder and decoder collapses to: \emph{place one point}. Every
theorem below measures the cost of that placement under a different notion of
cost---worst case, expected, up to symmetry, or along a product decomposition.

\section{Setting}

Throughout, $S$ is a finite nonempty set of configurations and $M$ a finite
nonempty set of records.

\begin{definition}[Observer, decoder, rate]
An \emph{observer} is a map $\obs : S \to M$ and a \emph{decoder} a map
$\dec : M \to S$. The \emph{rate} is $\rate(\obs) = |\{\obs(s): s\in S\}|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Distortion]
A \emph{distortion} is a map $d : S\times S\to\mathbb{N}$; $d(c,s)$ penalizes
reconstructing $s$ as $c$. Neither symmetry nor a triangle inequality is assumed.
On $S=\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$ the \emph{Hamming distortion} is
$d_H(c,s)=|\{i : c_i\ne s_i\}|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Perfect privacy]
An observer is \emph{perfectly private} if $\obs(s)=\obs(t)$ for all $s,t\in S$.
\end{definition}

Perfect privacy forces $\rate(\obs)=1$, and conversely on a nonempty $S$. Rate is
therefore the natural quantitative relaxation of privacy, and all converses below
are stated with rate as a parameter.

\begin{definition}[One-codeword covering radius]
$R(d) = \min_{c\in S}\max_{s\in S} d(c,s)$. Equivalently $R(d)\le D$ iff some ball
$B(c,D)=\{s: d(c,s)\le D\}$ is all of $S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp private threshold]\label{thm:sharp}
A budget $D$ is achievable by a perfectly private observer and decoder with
almost-sure distortion at most $D$ if and only if some $c\in S$ satisfies
$d(c,s)\le D$ for all $s$, i.e.\ iff $D\ge R(d)$. The same characterization holds
for randomized private channels.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A private observer is constant with value $m_0$, so $\dec(m_0)$ is a center;
conversely a center yields a constant observer and decoder. In the randomized
case, some record has positive probability and worst-case correctness of the
mixture forces worst-case correctness of the corresponding reconstruction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Binary Hamming threshold]\label{thm:hamR}
On $S=\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$, $R(d_H)=|\alpha|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\le$ is trivial; $\ge$ follows because the complement of any center is at
distance $|\alpha|$. Section~\ref{sec:tensor} gives a second, independent proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fibre-covering counting converse]\label{thm:count}
If $\dec(\obs(s))$ is within distortion $D$ of $s$ for all $s$ and every ball of
radius $D$ has at most $V$ elements, then $|S|\le \rate(\obs)\cdot V$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition $S$ into the fibres $\obs^{-1}(m)$; each fibre lies inside
$B(\dec(m),D)$ and there are $\rate(\obs)$ of them.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Exact Hamming ball volume]\label{prop:ball}
In $\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$ with $n=|\alpha|$, $|B(c,D)|=\sum_{i=0}^{D}\binom{n}{i}$ for
every center $c$.
\end{proposition}

Combining, $2^{n}\le\rate\cdot\sum_{i\le D}\binom{n}{i}$: reconstruction at
Hamming radius $D$ requires at least $n-\log_2\sum_{i\le D}\binom{n}{i}\approx
n(1-h(D/n))$ transmitted bits, $h$ the binary entropy function.

\section{Average-case theory: the private rate--distortion function}

Let $p : S\to\mathbb{R}$ be a source law.

\begin{definition}
The expected distortion of a single reconstruction $c$ is
$\avg(p,d,c)=\sum_s p(s)\,d(c,s)$; the system expected distortion of an
observer/decoder pair is $\sum_s p(s)\, d(\dec(\obs(s)),s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A budget $D\in\mathbb{R}$ is \emph{privately achievable on average} if some
perfectly private observer and decoder have system expected distortion at most
$D$. It is \emph{randomized-privately achievable on average} if there are a
channel $\mathrm{ch}: S\to(M\to\mathbb{R})$ with $\mathrm{ch}(s,m)\ge 0$,
$\sum_m \mathrm{ch}(s,m)=1$ and $\mathrm{ch}(s)=\mathrm{ch}(t)$ for all $s,t$, and
a decoder, with $\sum_s p(s)\sum_m \mathrm{ch}(s,m)\,d(\dec(m),s)\le D$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Deterministic average-case threshold]
$D$ is privately achievable on average iff some single $c$ has $\avg(p,d,c)\le D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A constant observer makes $\dec(\obs(s))$ the same point $c$ for every $s$, so the
system expected distortion equals $\avg(p,d,c)$ term by term; the converse is the
constant pair.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Randomization does not help on average]\label{thm:randavg}
$D$ is randomized-privately achievable on average iff some single $c$ has
$\avg(p,d,c)\le D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $q_m$ be the state-independent record law. Exchanging summations,
\[
\sum_s p(s)\sum_m q_m\, d(\dec(m),s) \;=\; \sum_m q_m\, \avg(p,d,\dec(m)),
\]
a convex combination since $q\ge 0$ and $\sum_m q_m=1$; a convex combination is at
least its minimum, so the best decoder output $\dec(m^\star)$ is at least as good.
No property of $p$ is used.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:randavg} is not a restatement of Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}: the
worst-case argument is about supports, this one about convexity.

\begin{definition}[Private rate--distortion function]
$\Dpriv(p,d)=\min_{c\in S}\avg(p,d,c)=\min_{c}\Emean_p[d(c,X)]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Optimality]
$\Dpriv(p,d)$ is the least privately-achievable-on-average budget.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Averaging never costs more]
If $p$ is a probability law then $\Dpriv(p,d)\le R(d)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Evaluate at a center realizing $R(d)$ and use $\sum_s p(s)=1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Exact solution for Hamming distortion}

Let $S=\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$.

\begin{definition}
$\mass_p(i,b)=\sum_{x:\,x_i\ne b}p(x)=\mathbb{P}_p[X_i\ne b]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Coordinate decomposition]\label{lem:coord}
$\avg(p,d_H,c)=\sum_{i\in\alpha}\mass_p(i,c_i)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $d_H(c,x)=\sum_i \mathbf 1[x_i\ne c_i]$, multiply by $p(x)$ and exchange the
sums.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Majority vote]
$\maj_p(i)=0$ if $\mass_p(i,0)\le\mass_p(i,1)$ and $1$ otherwise; then
$\mass_p(i,\maj_p(i))=\min(\mass_p(i,0),\mass_p(i,1))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact private rate--distortion function, Hamming]\label{thm:maj}
\[
\Dpriv(p,d_H)=\sum_{i\in\alpha}\min\big(\mass_p(i,0),\mass_p(i,1)\big),
\]
attained by the coordinatewise majority vote.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper bound: Lemma~\ref{lem:coord} at $c=\maj_p$. Lower bound: for any $c$ each
summand is one of the two values being minimized. Additivity of the distortion
together with a full product reconstruction alphabet makes the minimization
separate completely.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
In $\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$, for every $i$ and $b$ exactly half of the $2^{|\alpha|}$
tensors satisfy $x_i\ne b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Flipping the $i$-th coordinate is an involutive bijection between the two sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:unif}
Under the uniform law $\mass(i,b)=1/2$ for all $i,b$, hence
$\Dpriv(\unif,d_H)=|\alpha|/2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Strict separation]
If $\alpha\ne\emptyset$ then $|\alpha|/2 < |\alpha| = R(d_H)$: grading on average
buys exactly a factor of two and, by the previous theorem, never more than the
worst case. Operationally, a perfectly private observer (deterministic or
randomized) meets the expected Hamming budget $D$ against the uniform source iff
$D\ge|\alpha|/2$.
\end{corollary}

The value $|\alpha|/2$ is exactly the expected Hamming distance of a uniformly
random guess: perfect privacy leaves the observer no better than a coin.

\section{A measure-theoretic (Fano-type) converse}\label{sec:fano}

\begin{theorem}[Measure fibre-covering converse]\label{thm:measure}
Let $p:S\to\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $p\ge 0$, let $D\in\mathbb{N}$ and suppose every
ball has mass at most $\beta$, i.e.\ $\sum_{s:\,d(c,s)\le D}p(s)\le\beta$ for all
$c$. If $G\subseteq S$ satisfies $d(\dec(\obs(s)),s)\le D$ for all $s\in G$, then
\[
\sum_{s\in G}p(s)\;\le\;\rate(\obs)\cdot\beta .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
First $\beta\ge 0$, as it dominates the (nonnegative) mass of some ball.
Decompose $G=\biguplus_{m\in\obs(G)}\big(G\cap\obs^{-1}(m)\big)$ into disjoint
fibres. Each fibre is contained in $B(\dec(m),D)$; since $p\ge 0$, passing to a
superset only increases the sum, so each fibre has mass at most $\beta$. Hence
$p(G)\le|\obs(G)|\cdot\beta\le\rate(\obs)\cdot\beta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is the counting proof with the counting measure replaced by $p$: the same
inequality over two different semirings. Nonnegativity of $p$ is load-bearing---the
monotonicity step, and the theorem, fail for signed weights.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Fano-type excess-distortion converse]\label{thm:excess}
Let $p\ge 0$ with $\sum_s p(s)=1$, let every ball have mass at most $\beta$, let
$G$ be as above, and suppose $\sum_{s\notin G}p(s)\le\varepsilon$. Then
\[
1-\varepsilon \;\le\; \rate(\obs)\cdot\beta .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$p(G)+p(S\setminus G)=1$ and $p(S\setminus G)\le\varepsilon$ give
$p(G)\ge 1-\varepsilon$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:measure}.
\end{proof}

Reading $\log\rate$ as transmitted bits and $\beta$ as $\vol_D/|S|$, this is the
combinatorial skeleton of the classical rate--distortion converse.

\begin{lemma}[Uniform ball mass]
For every center $c$ in $\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$, $n=|\alpha|$,
$\sum_{s:\,d_H(c,s)\le D}\unif(s)=\big(\sum_{i\le D}\binom{n}{i}\big)/2^{n}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Concrete excess-distortion bound]\label{thm:concrete}
If an observer/decoder pair reconstructs every configuration outside a failure
event of uniform probability at most $\varepsilon$ within Hamming distortion $D$,
then
\[
(1-\varepsilon)\,2^{n}\;\le\;\rate(\obs)\cdot\sum_{i=0}^{D}\binom{n}{i}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Private observers]
A perfectly private observer has $\rate=1$, hence
$(1-\varepsilon)2^{n}\le\sum_{i=0}^{D}\binom{n}{i}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Failure probability]
A perfectly private observer working at Hamming radius $D$ satisfies
\[
\varepsilon\;\ge\;1-\frac{\sum_{i\le D}\binom{n}{i}}{2^{n}}
\;=\;\mathbb{P}\big[\Bin(n,\tfrac12)>D\big].
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Strict binomial tail]\label{lem:strict}
If $D<n$ then $\sum_{i=0}^{D}\binom{n}{i}<2^{n}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\sum_{i=0}^{n}\binom{n}{i}=2^n$, all terms are nonnegative, and the omitted term
$\binom{n}{n}=1$ is strictly positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero excess forces full distortion]
If a perfectly private observer reconstructs \emph{every} configuration in
$\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$ within Hamming distortion $D$, then $D\ge n=|\alpha|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $\varepsilon=0$, $G=S$: $2^n\le\sum_{i\le D}\binom{n}{i}$, contradicting
Lemma~\ref{lem:strict} if $D<n$.
\end{proof}

This re-derives Theorem~\ref{thm:hamR} by a volume inequality rather than a
covering argument: the qualitative threshold is precisely the zero-slack corner of
a quantitative inequality with a continuous knob $\varepsilon$.

\section{Distortion modulo relabeling}\label{sec:relab}

Let $\Sym(\alpha)$ act on $\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$ by precomposition,
$x\mapsto x\circ g$, and put $\supp(x)=\{i: x_i=1\}$, $\wt(x)=|\supp(x)|$,
$n=|\alpha|$.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{orbit distortion} is $\orb(x,y)=\min_{g\in\Sym(\alpha)}d_H(x\circ g, y)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact orbit distance]\label{thm:orb}
$\orb(x,y)=\big|\wt(x)-\wt(y)\big|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Relabeling preserves weight and $\wt(u)\le \wt(v)+d_H(u,v)$ for any $u,v$; applying
this in both directions gives the lower bound. For the upper bound, for an
admissible target weight $k$ there is an indicator tensor $\mathbf 1_T$, $|T|=k$,
whose support is a subset or a superset of $\supp(y)$, so
$d_H(\mathbf 1_T,y)=|k-\wt(y)|$ exactly; transitivity of the relabeling action on
each weight class (extend a bijection of supports to a permutation of $\alpha$)
produces $g$ with $x\circ g=\mathbf 1_T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp relabeled private threshold]
$R(\orb)=\lceil n/2\rceil=\lfloor (n+1)/2\rfloor$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\le$: take a center of weight $\lfloor n/2\rfloor$, so every weight gap is at most
$\lceil n/2\rceil$. $\ge$: test any center against the all-zero and all-one
tensors, giving $R\ge\max(\wt(c), n-\wt(c))\ge\lceil n/2\rceil$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A perfectly private observer meets a relabeling-tolerant worst-case budget $D$ iff
$D\ge\lceil n/2\rceil$. For $n\ge 2$ this is strictly below $R(d_H)=n$: quotienting
by the full relabeling group buys exactly a factor of two and no more.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Exact orbit volume]
The set of tensors of weight $m$---a single orbit---has exactly $\binom{n}{m}$
elements.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The support map is a bijection onto the $m$-subsets of $\alpha$, inverse
$T\mapsto\mathbf 1_T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact orbit ball volume]
An orbit ball of radius $D$ about a center of weight $k$ is the disjoint union of
the weight layers in $[k-D,k+D]$, hence
\[
\big|\{s : \orb(c,s)\le D\}\big| \;=\; \sum_{m=k-D}^{k+D}\binom{n}{m}.
\]
\end{theorem}

This is the orbit-counting (Burnside) analogue of the Hamming ball volume: the
ball is a union of whole orbits with binomial sizes.

\begin{corollary}
$\big|\{s : \orb(c,s)\le D\}\big|\le (2D+1)\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative converse modulo relabeling]
Any observer/decoder pair achieving relabeled worst-case distortion $D$ satisfies
\[
2^{n}\;\le\;\rate(\obs)\cdot(2D+1)\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}.
\]
\end{theorem}

Since $\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}\sim 2^{n}\sqrt{2/(\pi n)}$, this forces
$\rate\gtrsim\sqrt{\pi n/2}/(2D+1)$: anonymity softens the geometry but does not
make disclosure free.

\section{Tensorization: privacy budgets add}\label{sec:tensor}

Let $\sigma_i$, $i\in\iota$, be finite nonempty sets, $S=\prod_i\sigma_i$.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{additive product distortion} is $d(c,s)=\sum_i d_i(c_i,s_i)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Tensorization of the private threshold]\label{thm:tens}
$R\big(\sum_i d_i\big)=\sum_i R(d_i)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\le$) Assemble a product center from componentwise optimal centers.
($\ge$) Let $c$ realize $R(\sum_i d_i)$. In each component there is $s_i$ with
$d_i(c_i,s_i)\ge R(d_i)$---otherwise $c_i$ would cover $\sigma_i$ at radius
$R(d_i)-1$, contradicting minimality. Gluing the witnesses gives a state at
distance at least $\sum_i R(d_i)$.
\end{proof}

Both nonemptiness and finiteness of the factors are used: an empty factor is
vacuously covered at radius $0$ and the witness-extraction step has nothing to
extract.

\begin{corollary}
A perfectly private observer of a product system meets the worst-case budget $D$
iff $D\ge\sum_i R(d_i)$: the private budget must be split across components and no
cross-component trade is possible.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}
The two-point distortion $d_{\mathrm{bit}}(a,b)=\mathbf 1[a\ne b]$ has
$R(d_{\mathrm{bit}})=1$, and binary Hamming distortion is the additive product of
$|\alpha|$ copies of it.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}
$R(d_H)=|\alpha|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine the lemma with Theorem~\ref{thm:tens}: $\sum_{i\in\alpha}1=|\alpha|$.
\end{proof}

This proof is structurally independent of Theorem~\ref{thm:hamR} (which used the
antipode), so the two cross-validate the constant.

\begin{corollary}[Time-sliced histories]
Viewing a $T$-step history as $T$ snapshots with additive per-snapshot Hamming
distortion, the private worst-case distortion is $\sum_{t<T}n^2=Tn^2$: the per-step
thresholds add along the filtration, so a per-step privacy budget is without loss
relative to a global one.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}
The $i$-th \emph{marginal} of $p$ is $\marg_i(p)(a)=\sum_{s:\,s_i=a}p(s)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Splitting of the expected distortion]\label{lem:split}
For any $p$, additive $d$, and any single reconstruction $c$,
\[
\Emean_p\Big[\sum_i d_i(c_i,X_i)\Big]
= \sum_i\sum_{a\in\sigma_i}\marg_i(p)(a)\, d_i(c_i,a).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand, exchange the order of summation, and group states fibrewise by the value of
the $i$-th coordinate.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Average-case tensorization]\label{thm:avgtens}
For an additive distortion on a product configuration space and \emph{any} source
law $p$---with no independence assumption---
\[
\Dpriv\Big(p,\ \sum_i d_i\Big) \;=\; \sum_i \Dpriv\big(\marg_i(p),\, d_i\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:split} the objective is a sum of terms each depending on a
single component of $c$, so the minimizations separate: use componentwise
minimizers for $\le$, and bound each component's term below by its own minimum for
$\ge$.
\end{proof}

Correlations across components---however strong---do not change the private
optimum, which sees only the marginals: a private decoder has exactly one
reconstruction to offer, an additive distortion evaluates it coordinatewise, and
expectation is linear. Theorem~\ref{thm:avgtens} subsumes Theorem~\ref{thm:maj}
via $\sigma_i=\{0,1\}$, $d_i=d_{\mathrm{bit}}$.

\section{Consequences for surveillance networks}

Let $\alpha=\{0,\dots,T-1\}\times\{1,\dots,n\}^2$, $|\alpha|=Tn^2$,
$S=\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Regime & Optimal private distortion \\
\midrule
Worst case, Hamming & $Tn^2$ \\
Worst case, time-sliced (additive per snapshot) & $\sum_{t<T}n^2=Tn^2$ \\
Expected, uniform source & $Tn^2/2$ \\
Worst case, modulo relabeling of participants & $\lceil Tn^2/2\rceil$ \\
Expected, arbitrary source, Hamming & $\sum_i\min(\mass_i(0),\mass_i(1))$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent For a non-private observer at rate $\rate$ and failure probability
$\varepsilon$ against the uniform source,
\[
(1-\varepsilon)2^{Tn^2}\le\rate\cdot\!\!\sum_{i\le D}\!\binom{Tn^2}{i},
\qquad
(1-\varepsilon)2^{Tn^2}\le\rate\cdot(2D+1)\binom{Tn^2}{\lfloor Tn^2/2\rfloor}
\ \text{(relabeled)}.
\]

Perfect privacy and worst-case fidelity are mutually exclusive; on average the
best private channel is exactly as good as a uniformly random guess; giving up
participant identities helps by a factor of two and stops there.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Optimal private reconstruction by majority vote.}
Compute $\mass_i(0),\mass_i(1)$ for each coordinate, output the coordinatewise
argmin and the objective $\sum_i\min(\cdot,\cdot)$. Complexity
$O(|\alpha|\cdot|\supp(p)|)$ with an explicit table, or $O(|\alpha|)$ per sample
in a streaming version; exact by Theorem~\ref{thm:maj}, with no search over the
$2^{|\alpha|}$ reconstructions.

\paragraph{(B) Binomial-tail converse evaluation.}
Compute $\vol_D=\sum_{i\le D}\binom{n}{i}$ by the Pascal recurrence in $O(nD)$
integer operations, test $(1-\varepsilon)2^n\le\rho\vol_D$, and report the minimum
feasible rate $\lceil (1-\varepsilon)2^n/\vol_D\rceil$ and the minimum feasible
failure probability $1-\vol_D/2^n$. Exact arithmetic avoids floating-point error.

\paragraph{(C) Orbit ball volume and the relabeled converse.}
Compute $\sum_{m=\max(0,k-D)}^{\min(n,k+D)}\binom{n}{m}$ in $O(D)$ additions after
$O(n)$ preprocessing, compare with $(2D+1)\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}$, and report
the implied rate lower bound $2^n/\big((2D+1)\binom{n}{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}\big)$.

\section{Discussion and open directions}

Three mechanisms recur: \emph{collapse} (privacy reduces design to placing one
point), \emph{fibres} (every converse is: partition by record, each fibre sits in a
ball, count), and \emph{additivity} (closed forms exist exactly where the
distortion is additive over a product with a full product reconstruction
alphabet). Perfect privacy is a boundary condition rather than a design target;
the rate parameter is the natural interpolation, and the measure converse controls
$p(G)$ rather than $H(X)-H(X\mid Y)$.

\begin{conjecture}[Entropy-sensitive converse]
For a source law $p$ and a channel with excess-distortion probability
$\varepsilon$,
$H(X)-H(X\mid Y)\ge H(X)-\log\vol_D-h(\varepsilon)-\varepsilon\log|S|$. The
converse of Section~\ref{sec:fano} is the counting shadow of this statement.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Relabeled average distortion]
For the uniform source on $\{0,1\}^{\alpha}$, $n=|\alpha|$,
\[
\min_c \Emean_{\unif}\big[\orb(c,X)\big]
= \min_{0\le k\le n}\Emean\big[|k-W|\big],\quad W\sim\Bin(n,\tfrac12),
\]
with common value $n\binom{n-1}{\lfloor (n-1)/2\rfloor}/2^{n-1}=\Theta(\sqrt n)$
rather than $\Theta(n)$. Theorem~\ref{thm:orb} collapses the minimization over
$2^n$ reconstructions to one over $n+1$ integers, so what remains is a binomial
mean-absolute-deviation identity.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Marginal determinacy fails for non-additive distortion]
For the max distortion $d(c,s)=\max_i d_i(c_i,s_i)$ on a two-component product,
the worst-case threshold is still marginal determined, $R(d)=\max_i R(d_i)$, but
the average-case one is not: there exist two laws with identical marginals and
different $\Dpriv$, the gap being controlled by a total-variation coupling distance
between $p$ and the product of its marginals.
\end{conjecture}

Further directions: replacing the single covering number by a sequential covering
number along the natural filtration for causal observers, and relating it to
directed information; a mixing-based deficit in the converse exponent for
Markov-generated histories, for which the ambient count $2^{Tn^2}$ is far from
tight; and orbit distortion under a proper subgroup $H\le\Sym(\alpha)$, where
volumes require genuine Burnside counting and the loss relative to the full group
should be controlled by graph asymmetry.

\section{Conclusion}

Perfect privacy transforms a coding problem into a covering problem: place one
point to be close to everything. For binary configurations of dimension $n$ the
cost is $n$ in the worst case, $n/2$ on average against a uniform source, and
$\lceil n/2\rceil$ in the worst case modulo relabeling; it adds exactly across
additive product components in both the worst-case and the average-case theory,
with correlations contributing nothing; and it degrades gracefully with a rate
budget and a failure probability through
$(1-\varepsilon)2^n\le\rate\cdot\sum_{i\le D}\binom{n}{i}$, whose zero-slack,
unit-rate corner is precisely the sharp threshold. Each relaxation of the contract
buys a factor of two, and no relaxation considered here buys more.

